    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Abbreviations, capitals and punctuation",
     "texto": [
      "**Abbreviations** shorten a word and end in a full stop: **Sr.**, **Sra.**, **Dr.**, **Ud.**, **pág.**, **tel.**, **avda.** (avenida), **c/** (calle), **p. ej.**, **etc.** They are read as the full word: **Sr.** is read **señor**. The plural adds **-s** (**págs.**, **Sres.**, **Uds.**) or doubles the letter when there is only one: **EE. UU.**, **JJ. OO.**, **RR. HH.**\n\n**Acronyms** are made of initials, without full stops: **RAE**, **AVE**, **DNI**, **ESO**, **ADN**, **ITV**, **DGT**. They take the gender of the main word: **la** ESO (la educación), **el** DNI (el documento), **la** RAE (la academia). They do not change in the plural: **los AVE**, never «AVEs». Some are read as a word (**el AVE**, **la RAE**) and others letter by letter (**el DNI**: de-ene-i).\n\n**Capital letters: fewer than in English.** These take a **small letter**: days and months (**lunes**, **enero**), languages and nationalities (**español**, **una chica francesa**), titles before a name (**el señor García**, **el doctor Ruiz**) and the words of a title after the first (**Cien años de soledad**). These take a **capital**: names of people and places, institutions (**el Museo del Prado**) and festivals (**Navidad**). Capitals keep their tilde: **Ángel**, **África**.\n\n**Punctuation.** **¿ ?** and **¡ !** open and close. Spanish prefers angle **quotation marks** **«like this»**. The **dash** (**—**) introduces each speech in a novel. A colon follows the greeting of a letter: **Querida Ana:** Numbers take a point for thousands and a comma for decimals: **1.500,75**. **Ellipsis** is always three dots. And there is no comma before **y** in a list: pan, queso y vino."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Acronyms, abbreviations and capitals",
     "texto": [
      "**Acronyms: gender and plural.** An acronym takes the gender of its main word and does not change in the plural.\n\n- **La** RAE (la Real Academia Española)\n- **El** AVE (el tren de Alta Velocidad Española) → **los** AVE\n- **El** DNI (el documento nacional de identidad) → **los** DNI\n- **La** ESO (la Educación Secundaria Obligatoria)\n\n**Abbreviations: full stop and plural.** They always take a full stop. The plural doubles the letter or adds -s: **EE. UU.**, **págs.**, **Sres.**\n\n**Capital or small letter.** Ask yourself what the word is: if it is a proper name (**Madrid**, **López**, **Navidad**), a capital. If it is a day, a month, a language or a nationality, a small letter: **el lunes**, **una profesora francesa**.\n\n**After the greeting**, a colon, and a capital or a small letter depending on the style: **Estimado señor López:**"
     ],
